The sequence $x_n$ in $\ell_1$ converges to $c$ in $\ell_1$, meaning $\|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. For $p > 1$, we analyze the $\ell_p$ norm of $x_n - c$. Since $x_n$ and $c$ are non-negative, we focus on their absolute differences. For each term, $|x_n(k) - c(k)|^p \leq |x_n(k) - c(k)|$ when $|x_n(k) - c(k)| \leq 1$. For terms where $|x_n(k) - c(k)| > 1$, the number of such terms is finite due to the $\ell_1$ convergence (as the sum $\|x_n - c\|_1$ must be small). Thus, for sufficiently large $n$, all terms satisfy $|x_n(k) - c(k)| \leq 1$, making $\|x_n - c\|_p^p \leq \|x_n - c\|_1 \to 0$. Hence, $\|x_n - c\|_p \to 0$.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{Yes}

